index.\", \"Movies_and_TV:training_branch_allocation\": \"Runtime training snapshot; null when unavailable, including after loading only a state_dict. Protocol records the supplied baseline's fixed paired allocation and sampled-prefix context, and explicitly distinguishes the intervention on unchanged sample_batch windows. Example branches are sampled with terminal probability 0.75. Terminal examples mask only the final nonpadding item; random examples retain 0.15 MASK-only selection and its fallback. Both branches use the same left-padded clean-window construction. Epoch and cumulative branch counts distinguish examples, valid targets, terminal-position targets and empty branches. Each active branch contributes 0.5 times its own target-mean cross entropy. Batch records provide its CE sum, exact target denominator, per-target coefficient 0.5/denominator, coefficient mass and objective contribution. Empty branches contribute zero without redistributing weight. CE sums are detached directly from the summed catalog loss before target normalization; normal floating point rounding still applies. Global CE sums divided by global target counts differ from the mean of batch objectives. Example allocation and coefficient mass are not gradient shares. Random examples can also supervise the final position. These measurements are not host validation metrics or paired-baseline results. The supplied training loop uses paired sampled-prefix terminal examples; replacing these with terminal masks on latest-history windows changes context construction as well as example allocation. Consequently this implementation cannot establish a probability-only effect relative to that supplied loop.\", \"Movies_and_TV:pos_neg_margin_mean\": \"Mean of (pos_logit - neg_logit) over valid positions; higher means clearer separation.\", \"Movies_and_TV:pos_neg_margin_p10\": \"10th percentile of (pos_logit - neg_logit); lower suggests many hard/confusing cases.\", \"Movies_and_TV:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_